    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d868-a1">

                <pb facs="1016" n="936" sameAs="frus:frus1902/pg936"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Count Lamsdorff to
                        Mr. Riddle.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Ministry for Foreign Affairs</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >First Department</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1902-07-15"><hi rendition="#italic">July 15</hi> (<hi
                                rendition="#italic">28</hi>), <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >1902.</hi></date></dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 3843.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Chargé d’affaires</hi>: In reply to your note of
                    the 1st (14th) July, I have the honor to inform you that foreigners desiring to
                    visit our possessions in Central Asia are no longer obliged to obtain special
                    permission, and that travelers may, in consequence, freely sojourn in those
                    regions, with the exception of the towns of Koushk, Termez, and Kerki, and the
                    custom-house stations situated on the Amu Daria above Kerki.</p>
                <p>Tchikichliar, Tchatly, the military posts on the Atrek and on the line from
                    Koushk to Ak-rabat, as well as the railway from Merv to Koushk, are likewise
                    closed to travelers.</p>
                <p>I take this occasion, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Count Lamsdorff.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
